WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.940
Water holds far less oxygen than air.

2
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:12.160
Yet a trout's gill can send its blood out at a higher oxygen partial pressure than

3
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:13.400
the water leaving it.

4
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.980
Your lung cannot do that with air.

5
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.300
Why not?

6
00:00:18.983 --> 00:00:23.423
Each gas in a mixture has a partial pressure, its share of the total pressure.

7
00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:27.703
Dry air at sea level is about one fifth oxygen.

8
00:00:28.863 --> 00:00:30.703
But partial pressure is not amount.

9
00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:36.123
Water left touching air reaches the same oxygen partial pressure.

10
00:00:37.343 --> 00:00:39.503
Yet each litre holds far less oxygen.

11
00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:44.243
Oxygen crosses an exchange surface only by diffusion.

12
00:00:45.703 --> 00:00:50.563
Molecules cross both ways, but the net flow runs from higher partial pressure to

13
00:00:50.563 --> 00:00:55.903
lower. So here, richer means higher partial pressure, not more oxygen.

14
00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:59.267
Fick's law gives three dials for speed.

15
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:05.307
More area, a steeper partial pressure difference and a thinner barrier all speed

16
00:01:05.307 --> 00:01:09.547
transfer. That is why gills and lungs are large and thin.

17
00:01:10.747 --> 00:01:14.507
They are moist because gases must dissolve before crossing cells.

18
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.960
Here, all along the surface, the water or air stays richer than the blood beside it.

19
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.620
Diffusion brings the blood toward it, never past.

20
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.820
So the leaving blood can at best reach the partial pressure it last touched.

21
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.120
Run water and blood the same way along a thin gill plate, a lamella.

22
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.860
The water starts rich and the blood starts poor.

23
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.440
Partway along, their partial pressures close in on each other.

24
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.080
The rest of the plate adds little.

25
00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.350
Now reverse the blood, so it runs against the water.

26
00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.310
Where will the leaving blood end up?

27
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:03.067
The blood about to leave meets water that has just arrived.

28
00:02:04.247 --> 00:02:08.427
All along the plate, the water stays a little richer than the blood beside it.

29
00:02:09.407 --> 00:02:10.907
This is countercurrent exchange.

30
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:16.350
With a large, thin surface, the leaving blood can beat the water leaving.

31
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.610
Running the same way, it never could.

32
00:02:20.683 --> 00:02:25.203
Fold the surface into a dead-ended sac, an alveolus in a mammal lung.

33
00:02:26.643 --> 00:02:28.263
Air goes in and out by the same tube.

34
00:02:29.543 --> 00:02:32.003
Each breath only partly renews the gas inside.

35
00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:35.177
Blood keeps taking oxygen out.

36
00:02:36.317 --> 00:02:39.077
So the sac's gas sits well below the inhaled air.

37
00:02:40.377 --> 00:02:42.917
Every capillary touches that same mixed gas.

38
00:02:44.237 --> 00:02:45.457
Blood can at best match it.

39
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.860
That answers the trout.

40
00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:53.040
In its gill, the leaving blood last touches fresh, incoming water.

41
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.900
In your lung, it last touches stale, mixed gas.

42
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.040
Fick's law and the flows of blood and water or air set how close the blood gets.

43
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.600
The flow arrangement sets what it last touches.

44
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.840
Breathing keeps that water or air fresh.
